Body:
The Google Vertex `fetch` hook previously loaded `google-auth-library` lazily. Now that `GoogleAuth` is a top-level import, this Node-only dependency is resolved/loaded even when the google-vertex provider isn’t used. Consider using a lazy `import()` here (the dependency is already declared in package.json) to avoid unnecessary overhead for non-GCP users.
```suggestion
          fetch: async (input: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) => {
            const { GoogleAuth } = await import("google-auth-library")
```

`addWorkspaceDirectory` throws plain `Error` for missing/non-directory paths. In the server this will be converted into a 500 response (since only `NamedError` / `HTTPException` are mapped), which contradicts the OpenAPI/SDK contract advertising 400/404 and will surface as an internal error to clients for invalid input. Please return a 4xx here (e.g., throw a `HTTPException(400, ...)`, or introduce a typed `NamedError` and map it to 400) and consider adding a server test asserting invalid paths return 400.
```suggestion
      if (!stat) throw new NotFoundError(`Directory not found: ${input.path}`)
      if (!stat.isDirectory()) throw new NotFoundError(`Path is not a directory: ${input.path}`)
```

When navigating to home via “New session”, this only `drafts.set(...)` when there is input, but never clears an existing draft when the prompt was emptied. If the user cleared the input and then triggers “New session”, the stale draft for that session will remain in `drafts` and be restored later. Mirror the session-switch behavior by `drafts.delete(route.data.sessionID)` when there’s no input (and only when `route.data.type === "session"`).
```suggestion
          drafts.set(route.data.sessionID, { input: current.current.input, parts: [...current.current.parts] })
        } else if (route.data.type === "session") {
          // Clear any stale draft when there is no current input
          drafts.delete(route.data.sessionID)
```

`{file:...}` substitution can be skipped incorrectly when the same token appears first on a commented line (because `findIndex` returns the first matching line, then the `//` check `continue`s). This can leave unsubstituted tokens in non-comment lines below. Consider iterating matches with positional offsets (or processing per-line with indexes) so the comment-skip logic applies to the specific occurrence being replaced, not the first line containing the same token string.
```suggestion
    const configDir = path.dirname(configFilepath)
    const lines = text.split("\n")

    for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
      const originalLine = lines[i]
      if (originalLine.trim().startsWith("//")) continue

      let line = originalLine
      const fileRegex = /\{file:[^}]+\}/g
      let match: RegExpExecArray | null

      while ((match = fileRegex.exec(line)) !== null) {
        const token = match[0]

        let filePath = token.replace(/^\{file:/, "").replace(/\}$/, "")
        if (filePath.startsWith("~/")) {
          filePath = path.join(os.homedir(), filePath.slice(2))
        }

        const resolvedPath = path.isAbsolute(filePath) ? filePath : path.resolve(configDir, filePath)
        const fileContent = (
          await Bun.file(resolvedPath)
            .text()
            .catch((error) => {
              if (missing === "empty") return ""

              const errMsg = `bad file reference: "${token}"`
              if (error.code === "ENOENT") {
                throw new InvalidError(
                  {
                    path: configFilepath,
                    message: errMsg + ` ${resolvedPath} does not exist`,
                  },
                  { cause: error },
                )
              }
              throw new InvalidError({ path: configFilepath, message: errMsg }, { cause: error })
            })
        ).trim()

        const replacement = JSON.stringify(fileContent).slice(1, -1)
        line = line.slice(0, match.index) + replacement + line.slice(match.index + token.length)
        fileRegex.lastIndex = match.index + replacement.length
      }

      lines[i] = line
    }

    text = lines.join("\n")
```

The `package.json` anchoring write swallows all errors (`.catch(() => {})`). If this write fails (permissions, read-only filesystem), Bun will still walk up to a parent `package.json`, and the original auto-install issue will persist with no diagnostics. Consider letting the error propagate, or at least emitting a warning to stderr so the failure isn’t silent.
```suggestion
    .catch((error) => {
      console.error(
        `Failed to create package.json at "${binPackageJson}":`,
        error,
      )
    })
```
